BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Coffee and Conversation
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260630T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260630T110000
UID:6a038a50e13d3b4603b41a3f@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Enjoy some light refreshments\, and informal discussion about 
 services in the neighbourhood. Get to know your local library staff and le
 t us know what information and resources you would like to access at the l
 ibrary and in the community.\nEach month we will be joined by a different 
 community organization.\nIn June\, we will be joined by Newcomer Women's S
 ervices.\nSnacks and refreshments are provided for all participants. Ingre
 dient lists will be available at the program.
LOCATION:City Hall
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a038a50e13d3b4603b41a3f
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Housing Help
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260908T123000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260908T163000
UID:69583000c719ac98c335c8cf@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Drop in and work one-on-one with staff from The Housing Help C
 entre (THHC) to determine your housing needs\, connect you to other govern
 ment services\, and to match you with landlords across the city. THHC work
 s with both individuals and families in the City of Toronto to find and ma
 intain affordable\, safe housing.\nTHHC can be contacted at 416-285-8070.
LOCATION:Malvern
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69583000c719ac98c335c8cf
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Festival Select: Eight Men Out (John Sayles\, 1988)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260727T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260727T203000
UID:6a2b02428d1b82ca9f793a6c@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join the Society and Recreation department of the North York C
 entral Library for monthly screenings of fiction and non-fiction films tha
 t push the boundaries of the medium. This month we are showing John Sayles
 '\nEight Men Out\n(1988).\nSynopsis (Audio Cine Films): The great Chicago
  White Sox team of 1919 is the saddest team to ever win a pennent. The tea
 m is bitter at their penny pincher owner\, Charles Comiskey\, and at their
  own teammates. Gamblers take advantage of this opportunity to offer some 
 players $ to throw the series (Most of the players didn't get as much as p
 romised.) But Buck Weaver and the great Shoeless Joe Jackson turn back at 
 the last minute to try and play their best. The Sox actually almost come b
 ack from a 3-1 deficit. 2 years later\, the truth breaks out and the Sox a
 re sued on multiple accounts. They are found innocent by the jury but base
 ball commissioner Landis has other plans. The eight players are suspended 
 for life\, and Buck Weaver\, for the rest of his life\, tries to clear his
  name.\nNo registration required.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Room 101
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a2b02428d1b82ca9f793a6c
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
